# ---------------------------------------------------------------- CLARIFICATION
CLAR = [('Where the numbers stand', ['The 16 September Master Development Budget totals **$89,084,591** — $1,202/SF on the 74,100 SF footprint, or $655/SF on the 135,953 SF the sheet divides by. Our reconstruction of the trade cost ties to your Trade Cost cell to **$0.00** across 309 line items, so every figure quoted here is from your workbook or from the RFEM takeoff.', 'Five arithmetic items in the workbook account for about **$11.8M** all-in and none of them is a re-estimate. The rest of the difference between us is method and programme, which is what the next two sections are about.']), ('Can we do a roof lift? — and what the steel takeoff actually prices', ['**Our scheme, stated plainly.** Keep the existing roof and lift it to the target clear height on the lifter’s towers, extending the columns beneath and cladding the new band. Build the stage on its own footings poured into the slab. Build the **production mezzanine** and the **VIP mezzanine** from the ground up on their own footings inside the envelope. Nothing hangs from the building. The roof carries weather, snow and itself, and nothing else — all production and rigging load goes through the stage and tower structure straight into the ground.', '**The RFEM model (Rev 38, VE1) does not model that scheme.** We read this off the member geometry, not off anybody’s description of it, and every number is reproducible from the Member Detail sheet: 124 of the 243 column members start at or below +2 ft — 450,114 lb, 62.4% of all column steel — which is a new full-height column off the slab, not an extension. Only 25 members start at the +20 ft existing roof line. All 517 truss members are phase NEW, between +56.5 and +64.5 ft. The level *Existing Roof / Lower (+20 ft and below)* carries **zero** members. The words lift, jack, shore, temporary, splice and underpin appear nowhere in 8,658 rows. The model also carries a 32,942 SF mezzanine at +20 ft and 13,077 SF of tiered seating of its own, plus a 26,389 SF **occupied terrace** at +45 ft 7 in — none of which is the production or VIP mezzanine we specified.', '**So the 997.2 short tons is the tonnage of a full rebuild.** New full-height columns, a new trussed roof at +64 ft 6 in, an occupied terrace, and the model’s own mezzanine and seating. It is not the tonnage of a lifted roof with ground-supported stage and mezzanines, and it should not be priced as though it were. The two schemes do not share a steel schedule: ours is a lifter’s contract plus column extensions plus the new wall band, with the stage, production mezzanine and VIP mezzanine priced as their own ground-up structures; the model’s is 997 tons of new frame. Applying $6,000/TON to 997 tons answers a question about a building we have not asked for.', '**What we are asking is simpler than a re-price: is it possible?** (1) Can the existing roof be lifted — in whole or in part — to the target clear height, with the stage, production mezzanine and VIP mezzanine standing on their own footings beneath it? (2) What is the existing roof made of? Line 024100-19 demolishes *74,000 SF of pre-cast concrete roof*, and a pre-cast plank roof is a far worse lift candidate than a steel-framed one; this one answer may settle the question on its own. (3) Will the structural engineer look at the lift as a scheme, rather than as a variation on the rebuild? (4) We will commission Rooflifters’ Stage 3 analysis — tell us what to hand them. We are not asking anyone to re-run tonnage or re-price anything until the answer to (1) is yes.', '**The middle option: a partial lift.** If the whole roof cannot be lifted, the **27,393 SF high-roof zone** over the house is one clear-span plane with nothing stacked on it, and it is the zone where our ground-supported structure asks nothing of the roof. The terrace zone, if it survives the programme decision, is a floor with people on it and is a rebuild regardless. A partial lift would need the existing roof to survive being cut free and jacked, to carry snow and drift at the new height, and to meet a rebuilt terrace and lower roof at two new structural interfaces. Those are questions for the structural engineer, and we would rather ask them in two weeks than carry them into DD.', '**What a lift does and does not save.** Three things worth being clear about so nobody expects the wrong result. First, the ground-up stage and VIP decking do not by themselves take steel out of the roof — the model already assumes the roof carries no rigging, which is why the high roof is only 8.65 lb/SF, and our ground-supported structure is the reason it can be that light. That saving is already banked. Second, what a partial lift over the house avoids is the new high-roof steel (118.5 tons), its deck, its membrane and the demolition of that zone, against which sits the lifter’s contract, the column extensions and the temporary works — on cost that is close to a wash, and the case for it is weather exposure, a smaller demolition and possibly the DOB route, not the steel. Third, the steel that actually costs money is the columns and bracing, 359 tons and 37% of the building, driven by 60 ft of column height and by carrying the occupied terrace. If the aim is materially less steel, the levers are the programme (the terrace and the third floor) and the roof height (whether the clear height really needs +64 ft 6 in), and those move far more tonnage than any lift does.', '**So the sequence we propose:** (1) confirm what the existing roof is made of — if it is pre-cast, the lift is probably dead and we stop spending time on it; (2) settle the terrace and the height with Nate, because that is where the steel is; (3) if the roof turns out to be liftable, take the partial lift for the schedule and exposure benefits, with whatever steel it saves as a bonus.', '**If the answers come back pre-cast and no**, we will adopt the rebuild without further argument — and price it then. Until that answer is in, the 997 tons is a number about a different building.']), ('What else the takeoff settles', ['**Units.** The file reports 904.62 in a tonnes column, which is metric. US steel is bought in short tons at 2,000 lb, which makes it 997.2. Pricing off the tonnes column understates the steel by 10.2%.', '**The roof carries no rigging in the model either.** The high roof is 8.65 lb/SF of short-span W12 and W6 framing — weather only. Please confirm in writing that the roof design loads exclude production rigging, hoists and point loads, so a rigging allowance is not quietly added back at DD and paid for twice. Separately, the lower roof at 20.81 lb/SF is the heaviest per square foot in the building, heavier than the occupied terrace; if that is dunnage or screening it should say so.', '**Where the steel actually is.** Shared columns and vertical bracing: 718,634 lb, 37% of the whole. That weight is driven by 60 ft of column height and by carrying the occupied terrace, not by the roof — which is why the programme decision and the roof decision have to be made together.', '**One rate flag.** 80 of the columns (202 tons) are shop-welded built-up plate girders, I 48/24 through I 69.023/24, 60 LF each on grids B and E. $6,000/TON is a rolled-shape rate and will be low for these.']), ('Two corrections we are taking back', ['**135,953 SF.** We had called it wrong. The model carries 146,508 SF gross and 120,119 SF of enclosed floor, and our own mezzanine assumption was smaller than what the model draws. We can no longer prove your figure wrong — but it is unsourced, it does not derive from the test fit, and 555 Johnson at $208.63/SF is 134,993 SF. We are asking for its derivation and for one agreed area schedule, from PD04 Rev 4.4, that every budget labels.', '**The roof demolition line.** We had asked you to delete it. Withdrawn: it is the method the model uses. Whether it is the method we use is the section above, and that is ours to settle with Nate and the structural engineer, not yours.']), ('What still needs fixing — about $11.8M all-in', ['**Contingency, $6,590,876.** D67 computes 10% of the whole project on top of $5,786,479 of contractor contingency already inside D31. As labelled it would be $1,507,723; $578,648 of it is contingency on contingency.', '**General conditions twice, $2,643,751.** $3,141,000 staffing chart plus $3,034,000 of division 013000 inside trade = 12.36% of trade. Schimenti ran 7.80% and 8.90% at 555 Johnson. *Project Labor $2,000,000* has no crew, rate or duration behind it.', '**Sidewalk bridge, $1,859,913.** $4,000,000 lump; the backup rows beneath it foot to $2,589,800. The $2,000,000 of interior scaffolding we are no longer contesting outright — but the ground-supported stage and towers go up first and could carry the access.', '**Lighting bought twice, $2,151,701.** $1,750,000 in FF&E plus $1,631,436 of *I/O lighting package — provided by others* inside electrical.', '**Coat check lockers, $972,689.** 2,000 at $500 serves 27% of the house at once.', '**And D26, the $12,000,000 plug, is too small.** At your own $6,000/TON the model’s hot-rolled tonnage alone is roughly $5.9M before the plate-girder premium — and the plug still has to cover every bar, every door, storefront, glazing, tile, fireproofing and most of the drywall. It rises when priced, on either scheme.']), ('Why the fee structure matters', ['The 9% is currently earned on your own general conditions ($555,750 of fee, $672,458 all-in) and on the $12M of unpriced scope ($1,080,000, $1,306,800 all-in). Profit on the cost of your own staff is double-dipping; fee on a plug rises automatically when the plug is priced. A fixed fee removes both. Insurance is a quotable premium, not a margin — and under NY Labor Law 240 the owner is exposed whether or not the contractor holds the policy, so the only question is who buys it and at what markup.']), ('Deal structure, for the record', ['EBC owns the land; we sign a 30-year lease; Insomniac funds and builds out. Everything here is a leasehold improvement that reverts at the end of the term. The largest untested lever in the whole exercise is what EBC contributes to a build on its own land. Nobody has asked yet.'])]
